WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.230
In this video, you ask Liz, the AI assistant in Rancher, to troubleshoot a virtual machine, read a log, and create a new VM on SUSE Virtualization.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:17.950
This is the harvester-ai cluster inside Rancher. Filter the virtual machine list for the two demo VMs.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:24.030
The VM demo-liz-app does not start. It stays Pending, and the list does not show the reason.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:31.270
Open Liz from the header. Liz calls MCP tools that read and change resources with your own Rancher permissions.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:39.620
Choose the Virtualization agent. Its tools list and inspect VMs, volumes, images, and networks, and create or delete VMs.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:48.380
Ask Liz why demo-liz-app does not start, and name the namespace and the cluster.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:54.050
Each button is a resource that one of Liz's tool calls returned, so you can open exactly what it looked at.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:01:02.400
Liz reads the VM conditions and the volume list. The VM's disk points at a volume, demo-liz-app-data, that does not exist.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:10.140
The Virtualization agent has no log tool, so switch to the Rancher agent. Its inspect pod tool returns a pod's log.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:20.840
Ask for the log of the virt-launcher pod, the pod that runs the VM demo-liz-web.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:27.590
Liz finds the pod, reads its log, and summarizes how the VM booted and whether any lines point to a problem.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:31.820
To create a VM, switch back to the Virtualization agent.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:45.280
Describe the VM you want, with one CPU, two gigabytes of memory, a ten gigabyte disk, an Ubuntu image, and a VM network.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:53.370
Creating a VM changes the cluster, so Liz stops and shows what the create tool will apply. Nothing happens until you confirm.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:02:00.080
Confirm the request to let Liz create the virtual machine.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.550
Liz reports the new VM with its size, disk, image, and network.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:10.060
Close Liz and check the result where it matters, in the virtual machine list of the cluster.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:16.500
The list now shows demo-liz-new, the VM that Liz created. It is Running.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:25.482
Liz works through MCP tools with your Rancher permissions and asks before it changes anything. Always check its answers against the cluster.
